    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d16">

                <pb facs="0104" n="938" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg938"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 656.119/122</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-01-05T17:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 5, 1918, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 11.15 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8161. Following for War Trade Board from Sheldon:</p>
                <p>No. 3. Referring Frothingham’s telegram to War Trade Board, No. 34, December
                        4.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d16-f1"/> I hope early decision will be reached
                    regarding discontinuance or otherwise navicerts as affecting Dutch tonnage to
                    lift their own rations under part [<hi rendition="#italic">provisional?</hi>]
                    Dutch agreement. Tonnage proposals for ultimate agreement presume continuance of
                    Halifax visits which Dutch seem to object to. While calls at Halifax ought not
                    to be objected to by Dutch and for [<hi rendition="#italic">under?</hi>]
                    provisional <hi rendition="#italic">modus operandi</hi>, the question is more
                    serious for tonnage arrangements under definite agreement. If the system of
                    navicerts be not abandoned it should be understood for purposes of American
                    prestige in Holland that system is approved by Allies and not dictated solely by
                    His Majesty’s Government. An early reply will be appreciated.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Page</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
